69:**Operations:** `get(ID)`, `activity(ID, cursor?, limit?, order?, kinds?, view?)`, `list(filters...)`, `count(filters...)`, `summary()`, `plan(scope?, mode=?, active=?)`, `agents(stale=N?)`, `models(agent?)`, `project_config(view?, role?, agent?)`, `whoami()`, `schema(operation?|mutation?)`
71:**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue. Element mutations in one batch run inside one activity transaction: when any statement is refused, the refused statements keep their own errors, every statement that would otherwise have succeeded is reported as `ok:false` with code `batch_rolled_back`, and nothing is persisted. A clean batch commits every statement and records one activity event per affected element, so a batch may change an element's checklist only once; issue further checklist changes as separate commands. Local and remote mutation commands both print that complete envelope before returning a nonzero exit, so the envelope always describes exactly what was persisted.
149:`schema(operation=NAME)` and `schema(mutation=NAME)` return one flat signature object: `kind`, `name`, a typed `signature`, description, parameters, and examples; mutations also report `destructive` and `idempotent`. They do not include unrelated fields, presets, operations, or mutation contracts. Provide exactly one scope. `schema()` without a scope retains the full legacy response.
183:The scoped signature includes `syntax`: the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument, and subsequent arguments use `key=value`. Thus `get(TASK-260907-bg79el)` matches `get(id:string)`.
225:Every id-first mutation accepts `id=...` (bare or quoted) as well as the first positional argument. This includes `link`, `add_checklist_item`, and `set_status`; supplying both forms is rejected. These successful dry runs preview without persisting changes.
